This page contains no mathematics: no derivations, formulas or computations. It holds four non-mathematical items, apparently from the late 1930s.

(1) A pasted newspaper clipping in Fraktur, 'Eine Lanze für die Alten' ('A lance for the old'). It argues that age is no obstacle to achievement and cites:
- Liszt (over 70 when he completed his best compositions) and Gounod (likewise);
- Rossini (aged 72 when he wrote the 'Messe Solenelle', i.e. the Petite messe solennelle);
- Gladstone (still steering the 'ship of state' at 80);
- Bismarck (already 52 when he 'organised the Deutscher Bund');
- Ito Hirobumi, Japan's 'greatest statesman' (began sweeping reforms at 65).
Hilbert was in his mid-70s at the time, which plausibly explains why he kept it.

(2) A bibliographic note: Heinrich von Eicken, 'Geschichte und System der mittelalterlichen Weltanschauung', Stuttgart: Cotta 1887. Hilbert's parenthetical gloss reads '(Religion als Quelle des Unglücks)', i.e. religion as a source of misfortune. This fits the planned book 'Philosophie auf naturwissenschaftlicher Grundlage' mentioned on the previous page.

(3) A list of Hilbert's own academic honours:
- 'In Helsingfors bin ich Ehrenmitglied' (honorary member in Helsinki);
- the ditto marks expand to 'In Zürich bin ich Ehrendoktor' (honorary doctor in Zurich);
- 'Danzig ?' marks an uncertain further honour in Danzig.

(4) A list headed 'Hitlers Verdienste' (Hitler's merits). It names:
- abolition of the many states in Germany (Gleichschaltung of the Länder, 1933–34);
- placing 5 million unemployed in work, with a parenthetical 'ev. Reduktion auf 1 Million', i.e. possibly a reduction to 1 million;
- removal of the injustices ('Unbillen') of the Versailles Treaty;
- introduction of general conscription (March 1935).

Dating: the previous page says '20 Jahre nach Kriegsschluss', which places these notes around 1938. It is not clear from the page whether this is Hilbert's own assessment, a summary of common opinion, or notes for a conversation. No mathematical content is to be reconstructed.
